### What is the Prototype pattern?

Prototype answers "I need another object just like this one." Rather than calling a complicated constructor and reconfiguring everything, you copy a prototype that is already set up.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class Account {
    private String accountId;
    private BigDecimal balance;
    private List<String> transactionIds;

    public Account(String accountId, BigDecimal balance, List<String> transactionIds) {
        this.accountId = accountId;
        this.balance = balance;
        this.transactionIds = new ArrayList<>(transactionIds);
    }

    // Copy constructor – deep copy
    public Account(Account other) {
        this.accountId = other.accountId;
        this.balance = other.balance;
        this.transactionIds = new ArrayList<>(other.transactionIds);
    }

    public void addTransaction(String txnId) {
        transactionIds.add(txnId);
    }

    public List<String> getTransactionIds() {
        return List.copyOf(transactionIds);
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return accountId + " bal=" + balance + " txns=" + transactionIds;
    }
}
```

### What is the shallow-clone trap?

A shallow copy duplicates the top-level object but copies only the references. If an `Account` holds a `List<String>`, a shallow copy gives you a new Account whose list field points to the very same list.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class ShallowCloneTrap {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> txns = new ArrayList<>(List.of("TXN-001"));

        // Simulate shallow copy (just copy references)
        Account original = new Account("ACC-001", new BigDecimal("100000"), txns);

        // SHALLOW: shares the same list!
        Account shallowCopy = new Account("ACC-001", original.balance, original.transactionIds);
        // If transactionIds were the SAME reference:
        // shallowCopy.addTransaction("TXN-HACK");
        // original would also show TXN-HACK!

        // DEEP COPY: uses copy constructor
        Account deepCopy = new Account(original);
        deepCopy.addTransaction("TXN-002");

        System.out.println("Original: " + original);
        System.out.println("Deep copy: " + deepCopy);
    }
}
```

```text
Original: ACC-001 bal=100000 txns=[TXN-001]
Deep copy: ACC-001 bal=100000 txns=[TXN-001, TXN-002]
```

The deep copy has its own independent list. The original is untouched.

### Why is Cloneable problematic?

Joshua Bloch, in Effective Java, criticises `Cloneable` harshly:

- `Cloneable` is a marker interface with no `clone` method – it merely changes the behaviour of `Object.clone()`, which is confusing.
- `clone()` is declared on `Object` as protected and returns `Object`, so callers need casts.
- `clone()` does not run constructors, so it can produce an object in an invalid state.
- `clone()` cannot assign `final` fields, which conflicts with immutability.
- It throws the checked `CloneNotSupportedException` even when cloning is valid.
- The contract between `clone`, `super.clone`, and deep copying is fragile.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

// PROBLEMATIC: using Cloneable
public class AccountCloneable implements Cloneable {
    private String accountId;
    private BigDecimal balance;
    private List<String> transactionIds;

    public AccountCloneable(String id, BigDecimal bal, List<String> txns) {
        this.accountId = id;
        this.balance = bal;
        this.transactionIds = new ArrayList<>(txns);
    }

    @Override
    public AccountCloneable clone() {
        try {
            AccountCloneable copy = (AccountCloneable) super.clone();
            // MUST manually deep-copy mutable fields!
            copy.transactionIds = new ArrayList<>(this.transactionIds);
            return copy;
        } catch (CloneNotSupportedException e) {
            throw new AssertionError("Should not happen", e);
        }
    }
}
```

### Why is a copy constructor preferred?

A copy constructor is explicit, type-safe, respects `final` fields, and you control exactly what is copied.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class AccountSafe {
    private final String accountId;
    private final BigDecimal balance;
    private final List<String> transactionIds;

    public AccountSafe(String id, BigDecimal balance, List<String> txns) {
        this.accountId = id;
        this.balance = balance;
        this.transactionIds = List.copyOf(txns);
    }

    // Copy constructor: explicit, works with final fields
    public AccountSafe(AccountSafe other) {
        this.accountId = other.accountId;
        this.balance = other.balance;
        this.transactionIds = List.copyOf(other.transactionIds);
    }

    // Static copy factory (alternative form)
    public static AccountSafe copyOf(AccountSafe other) {
        return new AccountSafe(other);
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return accountId + " bal=" + balance + " txns=" + transactionIds;
    }
}
```

Benefits over `Cloneable`:
- Runs a real constructor, so the object is always valid.
- Works with `final` fields.
- No casts, no checked exceptions.
- Deep-copy logic is explicit and visible.

### When is clone acceptable?

For arrays, `clone()` is fine and idiomatic because arrays have a well-defined, efficient clone:

```java
int[] original = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
int[] copy = original.clone(); // perfectly fine for arrays
```

For genuinely immutable classes, copying is unnecessary: you can safely share the same instance since nothing can mutate it.

### What are the use cases for Prototype?

- **Expensive construction**: building an object requires a database call or heavy computation; cloning avoids repeating that cost.
- **Pre-configured templates**: keep a prototype with defaults and clone it for each use.
- **Runtime type selection**: copy an object of unknown concrete type without knowing the type statically.
- **Snapshotting**: clone captures the current state for what-if scenarios.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class PrototypeDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Template: pre-configured order
        AccountSafe template = new AccountSafe("TEMPLATE", new BigDecimal("0"), List.of("SETUP"));

        // Prototype: copy and customise
        AccountSafe rajesh = new AccountSafe(template);
        AccountSafe priya = AccountSafe.copyOf(template);

        System.out.println("Template: " + template);
        System.out.println("Rajesh: " + rajesh);
        System.out.println("Priya: " + priya);
    }
}
```

```text
Template: TEMPLATE bal=0 txns=[SETUP]
Rajesh: TEMPLATE bal=0 txns=[SETUP]
Priya: TEMPLATE bal=0 txns=[SETUP]
```

### How do you test that deep copy is correct?

A proper deep-copy test verifies that modifying the copy does not affect the original. This is the definitive test for the shallow-clone trap: mutate every mutable field in the copy and assert the original is unchanged.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class DeepCopyTest {
    static class AccountTemplate {
        String type;
        BigDecimal balance;
        List<String> features;

        AccountTemplate(String type, BigDecimal balance, List<String> features) {
            this.type = type;
            this.balance = balance;
            this.features = features;
        }

        // Copy constructor (deep copy)
        AccountTemplate(AccountTemplate source) {
            this.type = source.type;
            this.balance = source.balance;
            this.features = new ArrayList<>(source.features);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AccountTemplate original = new AccountTemplate(
            "SAVINGS", new BigDecimal("50000"), new ArrayList<>(List.of("ATM", "UPI")));
        AccountTemplate copy = new AccountTemplate(original);

        // Mutate the copy
        copy.features.add("CHEQUE");
        copy.type = "CURRENT";

        // Original must be unaffected
        System.out.println("Original features: " + original.features);
        System.out.println("Copy features: " + copy.features);
        System.out.println("Deep copy correct: " + !original.features.contains("CHEQUE"));
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Original features: [ATM, UPI]
Copy features: [ATM, UPI, CHEQUE]
Deep copy correct: true
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- The most common mistake is a shallow copy that accidentally shares mutable state.
- In modern Java, records with a compact copy helper reduce the need for manual clone logic.
- Bloch's criticism of `Cloneable` is a standard interview topic; know the five problems.
- For banking what-if scenarios, deep-copy an account to simulate transactions without touching the real one.
